{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788341623973,"sessionID":"ses_f9e8d1600ffeTOnADKxO6TVRTr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_5f0569d9b4a44a9485c2f2e3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Find RMSNorm reference kernels","prompt":"Search the workspace at /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack and /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc for any existing RMSNorm, LayerNorm, or normalization-related pyasc asc2 kernel implementations, golden kernels, or test examples. Also find the golden kernel abs_f16.py and any sigmoid reference. Look for:\n1. Any file containing \"rms_norm\" or \"RmsNorm\" in the skill-stack\n2. Any file containing \"layer_norm\" or \"LayerNorm\" in the skill-stack  \n3. The golden kernel abs_f16.py\n4. Any asc2 kernel that uses reduce_sum, rsqrt, or does per-row normalization\n5. Any asc2 kernel that uses a two-pass or streaming reduction pattern\n6. The sigmoid.py reference mentioned in task.md (search for it)\n\nFor each relevant file found, report the full path and a brief summary of the kernel structure (tiling, APIs used, reduction pattern). Read the most relevant files in full and report their complete content if they are short (<100 lines).\n\nBe very thorough - search across the entire workspace including skills/, integrations/, golden/, python/test/, tutorials/ directories.","subagent_type":"explore"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_f9e8b417dffeqyqzLcvFTRpBNV\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nI now have a complete and thorough picture of all normalization-related kernels, references, and patterns in the workspace. Here is my comprehensive report.\n\n---\n\n# Search Results: RMSNorm / Normalization Kernels and References\n\n## Summary\n\nThe workspace contains a rich set of RMSNorm and normalization references. Most importantly, **there is already a complete `rms_norm.py` submission module** plus a **prior candidate attempt**, **two RMSNorm golden kernels** (f16/f32), **two LayerNorm golden kernels** (f32/bf16) showing the identical two-pass reduction pattern, the **`abs_f16.py` golden**, the **`sigmoid.py` reference** named in task.md, and a **`foreach_norm.py`** demonstrating the cross-core `atomic_add` reduction pattern. The `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc` tree contained **no** matching files (all hits are under `pyasc-skill-stack`).\n\n---\n\n## 1. RMSNorm references\n\n### `golden/kernels/rms_norm_f32.py` (257 lines) — THE primary golden\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/golden/kernels/rms_norm_f32.py`\n**Structure:** Two `@asc2.jit(reuse_alloc=1)` kernels + host dispatcher, mirroring CANN's `KernelRmsNormRegBase` (tiling key 5000) and `KernelRmsNormRegBaseSplitD` (tiling key 2001).\n- **`rms_norm_full_row_kernel`**: loads `[1, num_cols]` per row into UB, `x.to(asc.float32)`, `sum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(x_row_f32 * x_row_f32)`, `inv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)`, then `out = x_f32 * gamma_f32 * inv_rms`, cast back on store. `num_cols` is `asc2.ConstExpr`.\n- **`rms_norm_split_d_kernel`** (two-pass streaming): seeds `sum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, tile_cols], 0.0, ...))`, then a **first loop** streams D in `tile_cols=64` chunks accumulating `sum_sq = sum_sq + asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32 * x_f32)` (loop-carried, NOT parallel), computes `inv_rms`, then a **second disjoint loop** re-loads tiles and writes `x_f32 * gamma_f32 * inv_rms`. Host zero-pads to multiple of 64 and divides by REAL `num_cols`.\n- **Dispatcher**: `rms_norm_launch` picks full_row when `num_cols * dtype_bytes <= 64 KiB` AND `num_cols % 8 == 0`, else split_d. `CORE_NUM=8`, `TILE_COLS=64`, `EPS=1e-5`, `UB_BUDGET_BYTES=64*1024`. Inputs MUST be `torch.Tensor` (numpy silently zeroed on C310). Test shapes `(8,256)` full_row and `(8,1055)` split_d.\n\n### `golden/kernels/rms_norm_f16.py` (249 lines)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/golden/kernels/rms_norm_f16.py`\n**Structure:** Identical two-kernel + dispatcher layout to the f32 golden. Differences: casts `x`/`gamma` tiles to `asc.float32` in-kernel before `reduce_sum`/multiply, casts output back to `x.dtype` before `asc2.copy_out`. Tolerances `atol=rtol=2e-2` (full_row), `5e-2` (split_d). Test shapes `(8,256)` and `(8,1055)`.\n\n### `integrations/cannbench/submission/cann_bench/rms_norm.py` (168 lines) — EXISTING SUBMISSION (critical)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/submission/cann_bench/rms_norm.py`\n**Structure:** Uses pyasc v2's **native `asc2.rms_norm` builtin** as the fast path plus a padded two-pass fallback. `_MAX_CORES=72`, `_MAX_TILE_ELEMENTS=4096`.\n- **`_rms_norm_kernel`** (native path): loads gamma once, grid-strides over `total_tiles` of `rows_per_tile` rows each, calls `asc2.rms_norm(x, gamma, epsilon)` directly; for bf16 it casts via f16 (`asc2.rms_norm(x.to(asc.float16), gamma.to(asc.float16), epsilon).to(asc.bfloat16)`).\n- **`_rms_norm_fallback_kernel`** (two-pass): the same streaming pattern as the golden split_d but with `real_shape` tail handling instead of host padding. Seeds `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, ...))`, first loop `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(xf * xf)`, `inv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(acc / cols + epsilon)`, second loop writes `x.to(f32) * gamma.to(f32) * inv_rms`.\n- **Dispatcher** (`rms_norm` public): `native_copy_aligned = (cols * x.element_size()) % 32 == 0`; `native_fp32_fits_ub = not (f32 and cols > 4096)`; falls back when unaligned or f32 overflows. `_fallback_tile_size` picks 16/128/512/1024/2048/4096/8192 by cols.\n\n### `integrations/cannbench/workers/runs/20260901_code_rms_norm/candidate_builtin.py` (168 lines)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/runs/20260901_code_rms_norm/candidate_builtin.py`\n**Structure:** Byte-identical to the submission `rms_norm.py` above — a prior run's candidate that uses the `asc2.rms_norm` builtin + two-pass fallback.\n\n### Task definition files\n- `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_122835/rms_norm-generate/task.md` (537 lines) — the active task: write `candidate.py` with public `rms_norm(x, gamma, epsilon=1e-6)`. Lists 20 eval cases (D from 2 to 8192, f16/f32/bf16, includes non-aligned prime tails, inf/nan/zero special cases). Embeds sigmoid.py as the structural reference and the full asc2 kernel contract.\n- `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/tasks/rms_norm/golden.py` (65 lines, <100) — torch golden: `torch.nn.functional.rms_norm(x, normalized_shape=gamma.shape, weight=gamma, eps=epsilon)`. (Full content already shown above.)\n- `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/tasks/rms_norm/{desc.md, proto.yaml, cases.yaml}` — operator spec, schema `rms_norm(Tensor x, Tensor gamma, float epsilon=1e-6) -> Tensor y`, and the 20 evaluation cases.\n\n### Other rms_norm mentions (not kernels)\n- `teams/pyasc-kernel-dev-team/AGENTS.md` line 146 lists the golden kernel set including `rms_norm_f16`, `rms_norm_f32`.\n- `.gitignore` lines 30-31 ignore `teams/pyasc-kernel-dev-team/kernels/rms_norm_f16/` and `rms_norm_f32/` (these team folders are **gitignored**, so no team-authored rms_norm kernel is checked in).\n- `skills/pyasc-api-patterns/SKILL.md` line 332 documents the RMSNorm pattern (two-kernel + host-dispatcher on C310; `asc2.rms_norm` builtin currently NOT used in the golden).\n- `capabilities.yaml`, `tests/tools/perf/*`, `evidence/*` reference rms_norm perf cells.\n\n---\n\n## 2. LayerNorm golden kernels (same two-pass reduction pattern, more complex)\n\n### `golden/kernels/layer_norm_v4_f32.py` (265 lines)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/golden/kernels/layer_norm_v4_f32.py`\n**Structure:** Two kernels + host N-D flatten dispatcher. LayerNorm adds mean subtraction + beta.\n- **`layer_norm_v4_full_row_kernel`**: uses **`asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32, 1, keep_dims=True)`** (axis-1 reduction keeping dims) for both `mean` and `sum_x2`, then `var = sum_x2/num_cols - mean*mean`, `rstd = asc2.rsqrt(var + epsilon)`, broadcasts via `asc2.broadcast_to`, `out = (x - mean) * rstd * gamma + beta`.\n- **`layer_norm_v4_split_d_kernel`** (two-pass streaming): seeds `sum_x` and `sum_x2` via `asc2.reduce_sum(zero_seed)`, first loop carries both `sum_x = sum_x + asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32)` and `sum_x2 = sum_x2 + asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32 * x_f32)`; second loop writes with `mean_tile = asc2.full([1, tile_cols], mean, ...)` and `out = (x - mean_tile) * rstd * gamma + beta`.\n- Dispatcher: `_use_split_d` returns True if `num_cols % 8 != 0` or `num_cols*4*6 > 64KB`. Host pads to ALIGN=8 (full_row) or TILE_COLS=64 (split_d). `F32_INTERMEDIATE_FACTOR=6`.\n\n### `golden/kernels/layer_norm_v4_bf16.py` (255 lines)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/golden/kernels/layer_norm_v4_bf16.py`\n**Structure:** Same as f32; bf16 in / f32 reduce. Note it uses `asc2.rsqrt` in full_row but `1.0 / asc2.sqrt` in split_d (inconsistency worth noting).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The golden kernel `abs_f16.py` (requested)\n\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/golden/kernels/abs_f16.py` (114 lines, full content shown above)\n**Structure:** Canonical elementwise template. `@asc2.jit(reuse_alloc=1)` `abs_kernel` with `asc2.global_tensor`, `asc2.copy_in`/`asc2.copy_out`, `asc2.abs(x)`. Grid-stride over `tile_per_block` tiles per core (`TILE_SIZE=128`, `CORE_NUM=16`). `tail_behavior=aligned_only` — requires size multiple of `TILE_SIZE * CORE_NUM = 2048`. Uses `numpy` inputs (no cube involvement, so numpy-zeroing hazard does not apply). Host `abs_launch` uses `np.empty_like` + `asc.ceildiv`. This is the simplest reference for the elementwise grid-stride tile loop and the module/launch/test layout.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The `sigmoid.py` reference (mentioned in task.md)\n\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/submission/cann_bench/sigmoid.py` (59 lines, <100, full content shown above)\n**Structure:** Pattern A grid-stride elementwise kernel — the structural template task.md says to copy.\n- `@asc2.jit _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr, out_ptr, size, num_tiles, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int])`: 1-D `asc2.global_tensor`, grid-stride `asc2.range(block_idx(), num_tiles, block_num(), unroll_factor=2)`, **tail handling via `real_shape=[n]`** (no host padding), f16/bf16 promoted to f32, `y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)`, cast back on store.\n- Host `sigmoid(x)`: calls `ensure_npu_platform()`, makes contiguous, `torch.empty_like(x)`, selects `_WIDE_TILE=3072` when `size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE` else `_NARROW_TILE=1024`, `cores = min(72, num_tiles)`, `kernel[cores](...)`. `_MAX_CORES=72`.\n\n### Related: `_pyasc_runtime.py` (41 lines, <100, full content shown)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/submission/cann_bench/_pyasc_runtime.py`\nThread-safe one-time `ensure_npu_platform()` that switches pyasc to NPU backend (honors `CANN_BENCH_PYASC_PLATFORM` override). Imported by all submission modules.\n\n### Related: `gelu.py` (250 lines, full content shown)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/submission/cann_bench/gelu.py`\nShows the **wide/safe tile fallback pattern** (try wide tile, catch `RuntimeError` UB overflow, cache choice), module-level precomputed constants (no `math.*` in jit), and the cancellation-free erfc Horner chain referenced in task.md. Directly relevant for the UB-overflow retry strategy and numerical-stability idioms.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Kernels using `reduce_sum` / `rsqrt` / per-row normalization (two-pass / streaming reduction)\n\n### `golden/kernels/batch_norm_v3_f32.py` (179 lines, full content shown)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/golden/kernels/batch_norm_v3_f32.py`\nPer-channel reduction over strided [N,C,L]. Uses `asc2.reduce_sum(blk, 1)` (axis-1), `asc2.rsqrt(var + eps)`, and **`gm_barrier=True`** on the loop-carried accumulator (`for n in asc2.range(N_SIZE, gm_barrier=True)`). Explicitly states it uses \"the same full-reduce->scalar primitive as the rms_norm golden\". Fixed shape `[32,64,64]`.\n\n### `golden/target/test_kl_div.py` (86 lines, <100, full content shown)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/golden/target/test_kl_div.py`\n**Streaming reduction with carried tile accumulator**: `acc_block = asc2.zeros([tile_length], ...)` then `for i in asc2.range(loop_count, ...): ... acc_block = acc_block + block_result` then `final_result = asc2.reduce_sum(acc_block)`. Shows `pad_value=0` on `copy_in`, `asc2.ceildiv`, `vf_fusion=True`, dtype-conditional branching inside jit.\n\n### `golden/target/test_reduce_sum.py`\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/golden/target/test_reduce_sum.py`\nReduction over rows/cols with `asc2.reduce_sum(block, 1)` / `asc2.reduce_sum(block, 0)` and `gm_barrier=True` loop-carried accumulators. (Very large parametrized test table; not read in full but grep confirmed the reduction pattern.)\n\n### `teams/pyasc-kernel-dev-team/kernels/reduce_sum_f32/kernel.py` (107 lines, full content shown)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/teams/pyasc-kernel-dev-team/kernels/reduce_sum_f32/kernel.py`\nUses the **older API** (`asc2.tensor`, `asc2.load`, `asc2.store`, `parallel=True` on `asc2.range`) — contrast with the golden/submission modules which use `asc2.global_tensor`/`asc2.copy_in`/`asc2.copy_out`. Shows the basic full-row `asc2.reduce_sum(row)` -> `asc2.full([1, out_pad], s, ...)` -> store pattern (`OUT_PAD=8` for 32-byte alignment).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Cross-core two-pass reduction via `atomic_add`\n\n### `integrations/cannbench/submission/cann_bench/foreach_norm.py` (211 lines, full content shown)\n**Full path:** `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/submission/cann_bench/foreach_norm.py`\n**The cross-core two-pass reduction reference.** Pattern: each core grid-stride reduces its tiles into a scalar `acc` (seeded `asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, ...))`), then `asc2.atomic_add(asc2.full([8], acc, ...), acc_gm, [0])` to combine across cores into a host-allocated 8-element f32 buffer (zeroed by a tiny `_zero_f32_kernel`). A second single-core `_finalize_*_kernel` reads `s = asc2.reduce_max(s_tile)` (to extract the scalar from the 8-wide buffer), applies the final transform (sqrt / exp(log(S)/p) / copy), and writes `[8]` with `real_shape=[8]`. Shows `_reduce_sq_sum_kernel` doing exactly `acc + asc2.reduce_sum(xf * xf)` — the RMSNorm sum-of-squares primitive.\n\n### Other `gm_barrier` / `atomic_add` usages (grep results)\n- `golden/target/test_broadcast.py`, `test_split_v.py`, `test_reduce_sum.py` use `gm_barrier=True` on loop-carried reductions.\n- `docker/pyasc-overlay/asc2/__init__.py` and `asc_language_tile/atomic_ops.py` define `atomic_add`/`atomic_max`.\n- `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/range.py` documents `gm_barrier` kwarg (note: task.md says `gm_barrier` is NOT available on the cannbench build — discrepancy to be aware of).\n- `integrations/cannbench/workers/runs/20260828_222246/foreach_norm-generate/iter{2,3}/candidate.py` — prior foreach_norm candidate iterations using the same atomic_add pattern.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Additional reference modules in the submission package\n`/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/submission/cann_bench/` also contains: `exp.py`, `masked_scale.py`, `mish.py`, `swi_glu.py`, `foreach_addcdiv_scalar.py`, `__init__.py` — all follow the same `ensure_npu_platform()` + `@asc2.jit` + grid-stride tile loop + `real_shape` tails + f32-internal-compute contract documented in task.md.\n\n---\n\n## Key takeaways for the RMSNorm candidate\n\n1. **The exact pattern already exists twice**: the golden `rms_norm_f32.py`/`rms_norm_f16.py` (two-kernel full_row + split_d, host dispatcher, host zero-pad to 64) and the submission `rms_norm.py` (native `asc2.rms_norm` builtin fast path + `real_shape`-tail two-pass fallback). The submission version targets the 950PR with `_MAX_CORES=72` (vs the golden's `CORE_NUM=8` on C310).\n2. **Two-pass streaming reduction idiom** (for non-aligned / oversized D): seed `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, ...))`, loop `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32 * x_f32)`, then `inv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(acc / cols + epsilon)`, then a disjoint second loop writes `x_f32 * gamma_f32 * inv_rms`. This is the verified loop-carried accumulator pattern task.md mandates.\n3. **Cross-core alternative**: `foreach_norm.py` shows the `atomic_add` combine pattern, but for RMSNorm each row's reduction is independent so the per-row two-pass (not cross-core atomic) is the right choice — rows are distributed grid-stride across cores.\n4. **Numerical stability**: f16/bf16 must compute sum-of-squares in f32 (`x.to(asc.float32)` before `x*x`); the golden and submission both do this. The bf16 submission path casts via f16 for the native builtin.\n5. **UB budget**: task.md gives ~253952 bytes with a 1.6x safety factor; the golden used 64 KiB on C310, the submission uses `_MAX_TILE_ELEMENTS=4096` and a `_fallback_tile_size` ladder.\n6. **Must match golden propagation** for inf/nan/zero special-value cases (cases 15-20 in task.md) — do not clamp.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_f9e8d1600ffeTOnADKxO6TVRTr","sessionId":"ses_f9e8b417dffeqyqzLcvFTRpBNV","model":{"modelID":"glm-5.2","providerID":"dashscope"},"truncated":false},"title":"Find RMSNorm reference kernels","time":{"start":1788341436036,"end":1788341623968}},"id":"prt_06174ac00001m21WDGse6sbxsV","sessionID":"ses_f9e8d1600ffeTOnADKxO6TVRTr","messageID":"msg_061731270001jmLm0nEc2v35wc"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788341788217,"sessionID":"ses_f9e8d1600ffeTOnADKxO6TVRTr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_aeaaf1d31d604997b5503711","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Search asc2 API patterns and constraints","prompt":"Search the workspace at /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack for documentation and examples of these specific asc2 API patterns. I need to understand the exact usage for each:\n\n1. `asc2.range` - How is it used for grid-stride loops AND for plain sequential loops? Can it be nested? What are all its kwargs?\n2. `asc2.reduce_sum` - How does it work? Does it reduce a tile to a scalar? What's the return type?\n3. `asc2.rsqrt` - Does it exist? What's the signature?\n4. `asc2.copy_in` / `asc2.copy_out` - Full signature including real_shape parameter\n5. `asc2.global_tensor` - 1-D and 2-D usage\n6. Loop-carried scalar accumulators - the exact pattern for accumulating reduce_sum results across iterations\n7. `asc2.atomic_add` - cross-core reduction pattern\n8. `asc.ConstExpr` and `asc.GlobalAddress` - usage in kernel params\n9. `asc.ceildiv` - usage\n10. Scalar operations inside @asc2.jit - can you do `scalar_a / scalar_b`, `asc2.rsqrt` on a scalar, etc.?\n\nSearch in:\n- skills/pyasc-api-patterns/\n- skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/\n- skills/pyasc-docs-search/\n- golden/docs/\n- golden/tutorials/\n- Any .md or .py files with relevant content\n\nRead all relevant SKILL.md files and reference docs fully. Report the exact API signatures and usage patterns found.","subagent_type":"explore"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_f9e8b2c21ffeVgKgdpbNcnvj6Q\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nI now have comprehensive information on all 10 API patterns from both the skill documentation and the actual source code. Here is my complete report.\n\n---\n\n# asc2 API Patterns — Complete Reference Report\n\nI searched the workspace at `/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack` thoroughly. The authoritative sources are:\n\n- **Skill docs**: `skills/pyasc-api-patterns/`, `skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/`, `skills/pyasc-docs-search/` (loaded via skill tool)\n- **Actual source code (NEWER API)**: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/*.py` — this is the current API with `global_tensor`/`copy_in`/`copy_out`/`gm_barrier`\n- **Older overlay source**: `docker/pyasc-overlay/asc_language_tile/*.py` — uses `tensor`/`load`/`store`/`parallel` (older API)\n- **Golden kernels**: `golden/kernels/*.py` — verified, working examples using the NEWER API\n\n> IMPORTANT: There are TWO API generations in this workspace. The NEWER one (`pyasc-v2-source`, used by all current golden kernels) uses `asc2.global_tensor` / `asc2.copy_in` / `asc2.copy_out` and `gm_barrier`. The older overlay uses `asc2.tensor` / `asc2.load` / `asc2.store` and `parallel`. The `pyasc-api-patterns` skill doc still shows the older `load/store` API and `parallel=` kwarg — but **the actual current code uses `copy_in/copy_out` and `gm_barrier`**. I report both and flag the discrepancy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. `asc2.range` — grid-stride, sequential, nested, and all kwargs\n\n### Signature (current/newer API)\nFile: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/range.py` (lines 60–79)\n\n```python\nclass range(BaseRange):\n    @overload\n    def __init__(self, stop: int, /, *, unroll_factor: int = 1, gm_barrier: bool = False): ...\n    @overload\n    def __init__(self, start: int, stop: int, /, *, unroll_factor: int = 1, gm_barrier: bool = False): ...\n    @overload\n    def __init__(self, start: int, stop: int, step: int, /, *, unroll_factor: int = 1, gm_barrier: bool = False): ...\n    def __init__(self, *args, unroll_factor: int = 1, gm_barrier: bool = False): ...\n```\n\nBaseRange (file `asc/language/core/range.py`) accepts positional `start, stop, step` (1–3 positional args), exactly mirroring Python's `range`. `step` defaults to `1`, `start` defaults to `0`.\n\n### Kwargs (complete list)\n| Kwarg | Type | Default | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `unroll_factor` | `int` | `1` | Loop unroll attribute on the emitted `ForOp`. Must be ≥ 1. Higher = more scheduling freedom. |\n| `gm_barrier` | `bool` | `False` | When `True`, inserts a global-memory barrier between iterations, **disabling** overlap of memory ops across iterations. Set `True` when an iteration reads a value a previous iteration wrote (loop-carried dependency). |\n\n> NOTE on `parallel` (older overlay): The older `docker/pyasc-overlay/asc_language_tile/range.py` uses `parallel: bool = False` instead of `gm_barrier`. They are logical inverses: `parallel=True` ≈ `gm_barrier=False` (overlap enabled). The skill doc's \"Pattern table\" still references `parallel`, but **all current golden kernels use `gm_barrier`** (e.g. `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:84`). Use `gm_barrier` in new code.\n\n### Plain sequential loop\n```python\nfor i in asc2.range(tile_per_block, unroll_factor=2):\n    ...\n# equivalent to: for i in asc2.range(0, tile_per_block, 1, unroll_factor=2)\n```\nFrom `rms_norm_f32.py:140`: `for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):`\n\n### Grid-stride loop (distributes work across cores)\nUses `start=block_idx()`, `stop=num_rows`, `step=block_num()`:\n```python\nfor row in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n    ...\n```\nSource: `rms_norm_f32.py:103`, `rms_norm_f16.py:103`, `layer_norm_v4_f32.py:76`.\n\n`asc2.block_idx() -> PlainValue` and `asc2.block_num() -> PlainValue` are defined in `asc2/language/prog_model_ops.py` (lines 16, 37).\n\n### Nesting — YES, supported\n`asc2.range` can be nested. Each call emits its own `ForOp`. The pattern in golden kernels: an outer row-distribution loop containing an inner tile loop:\n```python\nfor row in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_rows, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n    ...\n    for tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):   # inner, loop-carried\n        ...\n```\nSource: `rms_norm_f32.py:103` + `:140`; `layer_norm_v4_f32.py:114` + `:120`.\n\n> Note on compile-time loops: For loops whose trip count is a `ConstExpr[int]` (e.g. matmul m/n tiles), use plain Python `range(...)` — it is fully unrolled at JIT time. Wrapping such loops in `asc2.range(unroll_factor=2)` would emit a runtime `ForOp` and is a regression. Source: `pyasc-syntax-constraints` skill, \"Cube (matmul) loop + placement notes\".\n\n### `gm_barrier=True` example (loop-carried accumulator)\n```python\n# batch_norm_v3_f32.py:84 — sum_c/sumsq_c carry across n -> overlap must be OFF\nfor n in asc2.range(N_SIZE, gm_barrier=True):\n    blk = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [n * C_SIZE + c0, 0], [ch, L_SIZE])\n    sum_c = sum_c + asc2.reduce_sum(blk, 1)\n    sumsq_c = sumsq_c + asc2.reduce_sum(blk * blk, 1)\n```\n\nThere is also `asc2.static_range(start, stop, step)` (file `asc2/language/range.py:88`) — like `asc2.range` but requires all bounds to be **compile-time integer constants** (enables complete unrolling).\n\n---\n\n## 2. `asc2.reduce_sum` — full signature, return type, behavior\n\n### Signature (current API)\nFile: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/reduction_ops.py` (lines 70–115)\n\n```python\n@overload\ndef reduce_sum(input: LocalTensor, *dims: int, keep_dims: bool = False) -> LocalTensor: ...\n@overload\ndef reduce_sum(input: LocalTensor) -> PlainValue: ...\n\n@bind_tensor_method(name=\"sum\")\n@require_jit\ndef reduce_sum(input: LocalTensor, *dims: int, keep_dims: bool = False) -> Union[LocalTensor, PlainValue]: ...\n```\n\n### Return type — depends on whether dims are passed\n- **`asc2.reduce_sum(tile)`** (NO dims) → reduces the **entire tile to a single scalar** → returns `PlainValue` (a scalar/runtime value, NOT a tile). Implemented via `create_asctile_ReduceAs1dOp`.\n- **`asc2.reduce_sum(tile, dim, ...)`** (WITH dims) → reduces along the given axes → returns a `LocalTensor` (lower-rank tile). Implemented via `create_asctile_ReduceOp`.\n- `keep_dims=True` keeps reduced axes as size-1 (otherwise they are squeezed).\n\n### Supported dtypes\n- Multi-axis (dims given): `int32, int64, float32`\n- Scalar (no dims, \"1d\" path): `int64, float16, float32` — **so float16 tiles can be reduced to a scalar, but float16 multi-axis reduction is NOT supported** (must cast to float32 first).\n\n### Examples (from docstrings + golden kernels)\n```python\n# Reduce to scalar (returns PlainValue):\ninput = asc2.copy_in(x, [0, 0], [256, 256])\nresult = asc2.reduce_sum(input)         # PlainValue\n\n# Reduce along axis 0 (returns LocalTensor of shape [256]):\ninput = asc2.copy_in(x, [0, 0], [128, 256])\nresult = asc2.reduce_sum(input, 0)\n\n# keep_dims (from layer_norm_v4_f32.py:82):\nmean = asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32, 1, keep_dims=True) / num_cols   # LocalTensor shape [1, 1]\n```\n\nAlso available as a tile method: `tile.sum(*dims, keep_dims=False)` (via `@bind_tensor_method(name=\"sum\")`). Sibling ops: `asc2.reduce_max`, `asc2.reduce_min`, `asc2.reduce_prod` (prod does NOT support scalar).\n\n---\n\n## 3. `asc2.rsqrt` — EXISTS, signature and scalar limitation\n\n### Signature\nFile: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/unary_ops.py` (lines 222–227)\n\n```python\n@bind_tensor_method\n@require_jit\n@set_docstring(\"inverse square root\", support_dtypes=(KT.float16, KT.float32))\ndef rsqrt(input: LocalTensor) -> LocalTensor:\n    return op_unary_impl(input,\n                         global_builder.get_ir_builder().create_math_RsqrtOp,\n                         support_dtypes=(KT.float16, KT.float32))\n```\n\n### Key facts\n- **EXISTS.** Maps to `create_math_RsqrtOp`.\n- Input: `LocalTensor` ONLY. **Does NOT support scalars** (no `@overload` for `RuntimeFloat`, no `support_scalar=True`).\n- Supported dtypes: `float16`, `float32` only.\n- Returns a `LocalTensor` (same shape as input).\n\n### Scalar inverse-sqrt workaround\nSince `asc2.rsqrt(scalar)` is **not supported**, the golden pattern for scalar inv-rms uses `asc2.sqrt` (which DOES support scalars) and divides:\n```python\n# rms_norm_f32.py:108, rms_norm_f16.py:108 (sum_sq is a PlainValue/scalar):\ninv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)\n\n# layer_norm_v4_f32.py:129 (var is a scalar in split_d):\nrstd = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(var + epsilon)\n```\n`asc2.rsqrt` IS used when the operand is a **tile**:\n```python\n# layer_norm_v4_f32.py:86 (var is a LocalTensor [1,1] with keep_dims=True):\nrstd = asc2.rsqrt(var + epsilon)\n\n# batch_norm_v3_f32.py:91 (var_c is a [ch] LocalTensor):\ninvstd_c = asc2.rsqrt(var_c + eps)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. `asc2.copy_in` / `asc2.copy_out` — full signatures with `real_shape`\n\nFile: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/memory_ops.py`\n\n### `asc2.copy_in` (lines 125–227)\n```python\n@overload\ndef copy_in(src: GlobalTensor, offsets: Iterable[RuntimeInt], shape: Iterable[int],\n            location: TensorLocLike = TensorLocation.Auto, *,\n            real_shape: Optional[Iterable[RuntimeInt]] = None,\n            pad_value: Optional[RuntimeNumeric] = None) -> LocalTensor: ...\n@overload\ndef copy_in(src: GlobalTensor, offsets: Iterable[RuntimeInt]) -> PlainValue: ...\n\n@require_jit\ndef copy_in(src: GlobalTensor, offsets: Iterable[RuntimeInt], shape: Optional[Iterable[int]] = None,\n            location: TensorLocLike = TensorLocation.Auto, *,\n            real_shape: Optional[Iterable[RuntimeInt]] = None,\n            pad_value: Optional[RuntimeNumeric] = None) -> Union[LocalTensor, PlainValue]:\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `src`: `GlobalTensor` (from `asc2.global_tensor`)\n- `offsets`: per-dimension offsets into the global tensor (rank must match `src.rank`)\n- `shape`: shape of the local tensor to load. If `None`, loads a single scalar → returns `PlainValue`. Must be compile-time static (literal/`ConstExpr`). For 2D+ in UB, last dim must align to 32 bytes (8 for float32, 16 for float16).\n- `location`: `TensorLocation.Auto` (default), `UB`, `L1`, `L0A`, `L0B`, `BT`.\n- `real_shape`: keyword-only. Explicitly specifies how many elements to actually load per dim (rest filled with `pad_value`). Must match rank of `shape`; each dim ≤ corresponding `shape` dim. **Not supported for `L1`/`L0A`/`L0B`** (only `Auto`/`UB`).\n- `pad_value`: padding fill. If `pad_value` given but `real_shape` is None, `real_shape` defaults to `shape`. If `real_shape` given but `pad_value` is None, `pad_value` defaults to `0`.\n\n### `asc2.copy_out` (lines 230–318)\n```python\n@overload\ndef copy_out(src: LocalTensor, dst: GlobalTensor, offsets: Iterable[RuntimeInt], *,\n             real_shape: Optional[Iterable[RuntimeInt]] = None) -> None: ...\n@overload\ndef copy_out(src: RuntimeNumeric, dst: GlobalTensor, offsets: Iterable[RuntimeInt]) -> None: ...\n\n@require_jit\ndef copy_out(src: Union[LocalTensor, RuntimeNumeric], dst: GlobalTensor, offsets: Iterable[RuntimeInt], *,\n             real_shape: Optional[Iterable[RuntimeInt]] = None) -> None:\n```\n- `src`: `LocalTensor` (from `UB` or `L0C` only) OR a scalar (one-element tensor or numeric). For scalar stores, `real_shape` is **forbidden**.\n- `dst`: `GlobalTensor`.\n- `offsets`: per-dim offsets (rank must match `dst.rank`).\n- `real_shape`: keyword-only. Writes only `real_shape` elements per dim (rest unwritten). Must match `src.rank`; each dim ≤ corresponding `src` dim. Cannot be used with scalar stores.\n\n### `real_shape` examples (from docstrings)\n```python\nx_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [100, 100])\n# Load only 12x12 into a [16,16] tile, pad rest with -1.0:\nresult = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [0, 0], [16, 16], real_shape=[12, 12], pad_value=-1.0)\n\n# Store only 12x12 from a [16,16] tile:\nasc2.copy_out(src, out_gm, [0, 0], real_shape=[12, 12])\n```\n\n### Typical usage (no `real_shape`)\n```python\n# 1D (rms_norm-style, but RMSNorm uses 2D):\nx_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [1024])\nresult = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [256], [128])\n\n# 2D (the actual RMSNorm golden pattern, rms_norm_f32.py:105):\nx_row = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [row, 0], [1, num_cols])\nasc2.copy_out(out_f32.to(x_row.dtype), out_gm, [row, 0])\n```\n\nThere is also `asc2.copy(src, offsets=None, shape=None, location=None)` for **local-to-local** copies (e.g. `L1`→`L0A`, `L0C`→`UB`).\n\n> NOTE: The skill doc (`pyasc-api-patterns`) and older overlay still show `asc2.load(gm, [shape], offsets=[...])` / `asc2.store(tile, gm, offsets=[...])` (note **list** `offsets=` kwarg). The current code uses `asc2.copy_in/copy_out` with **positional** `offsets` (no `offsets=` kwarg). Use `copy_in`/`copy_out` for new code.\n\n---\n\n## 5. `asc2.global_tensor` — 1-D and 2-D usage\n\nFile: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/global_tensor.py` (lines 100–144)\n\n### Signature\n```python\n@require_jit\ndef global_tensor(base: GlobalAddress, shape: Iterable[RuntimeInt]) -> GlobalTensor:\n```\n- `base`: `GlobalAddress` (kernel param, e.g. `x_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress`).\n- `shape`: iterable of integer-like values (literals, `int` runtime params, or `PlainValue`). **Both static and dynamic dims supported.**\n- Returns: `GlobalTensor` (has `.dtype`, `.shape`, `.rank`).\n\n### 1-D usage\n```python\n# From docstring:\nx_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [1024])\n\n# From batch_norm_v3_f32.py:70 (1-D weight/bias):\nw_gm = asc2.global_tensor(w_ptr, [C_SIZE])\nb_gm = asc2.global_tensor(b_ptr, [C_SIZE])\nmean_gm = asc2.global_tensor(mean_ptr, [C_SIZE])\n```\n\n### 2-D usage\n```python\n# From docstring:\nx_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [64, 128])\n\n# From rms_norm_f32.py:99-101:\nx_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\ngamma_gm_2d = asc2.global_tensor(gamma_ptr, [1, num_cols])\nout_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])\n\n# Dynamic shape (runtime dims) is supported:\nx_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [num_rows, num_cols])   # num_rows/num_cols can be runtime int\n```\n\n> NOTE: The older overlay has `asc2.tensor(base, shape) -> Tensor` (same semantics, different name). The current API is `asc2.global_tensor`. The skill doc references `asc2.tensor` — treat as the older name.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Loop-carried scalar accumulators — the exact pattern\n\nThis is the **most important and subtle pattern**. Source: `rms_norm_f32.py:136–144`, `layer_norm_v4_f32.py:117–125`, `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:80–87`.\n\n### The problem\nYou cannot seed a loop-carried scalar with a plain Python `0.0` — the codegen rejects `sum_sq = 0.0` because the loop-carried value becomes a `PlainValue` of the wrong type, raising `'sum_sq' was re-assigned to an object with different type` (per `function_visitor.py`).\n\n### The required seed pattern\nSeed the accumulator with a `reduce_sum` of a zero-filled tile of the SAME rank/dtype you'll add inside the loop:\n\n```python\n# rms_norm_f32.py:136-144 (split_d branch, sum_sq is a PlainValue/scalar):\nzero_seed = asc2.full([1, tile_cols], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)\nsum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(zero_seed)              # PlainValue, seeded correctly\n\n# Inner reduction loop carries `sum_sq` -> NOT parallel -> use unroll_factor only,\n# do NOT set gm_barrier=False optimistically; for cross-iteration READ-AFTER-WRITE\n# through the accumulator, set gm_barrier=True (see batch_norm below).\nfor tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):\n    col = tile_id * tile_cols\n    x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [row, col], [1, tile_cols])\n    x_f32 = x.to(asc.float32)\n    sum_sq = sum_sq + asc2.reduce_sum(x_f32 * x_f32)   # PlainValue + PlainValue -> PlainValue\n```\n\n### Tile-valued accumulator variant (multi-channel)\nWhen the accumulator is a **tile** (one element per channel), seed with `asc2.full`:\n```python\n# batch_norm_v3_f32.py:80-87:\nsum_c = asc2.full([ch], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)       # LocalTensor seed\nsumsq_c = asc2.full([ch], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32)\n# Carried accumulator -> overlap must be OFF (gm_barrier=True):\nfor n in asc2.range(N_SIZE, gm_barrier=True):\n    blk = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [n * C_SIZE + c0, 0], [ch, L_SIZE])\n    sum_c = sum_c + asc2.reduce_sum(blk, 1)            # LocalTensor + LocalTensor\n    sumsq_c = sumsq_c + asc2.reduce_sum(blk * blk, 1)\n```\n\n### Two-loop split (accumulate, then disjoint write-back)\nThe RMSNorm/LayerNorm split_d pattern uses TWO loops: one to accumulate (gm_barrier=True conceptually, but the skill notes \"parallel omitted\" since the carry is through a scalar not GM), then a second disjoint write-back loop that IS safe to parallelize:\n```python\n# Loop 1: accumulate (carry across iterations)\nfor tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):\n    sum_sq = sum_sq + asc2.reduce_sum(...)\n\ninv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)\n\n# Loop 2: disjoint write-back (no carry -> safe to overlap)\nfor tile_id in asc2.range(num_tiles, unroll_factor=2):\n    ... asc2.copy_out(out_f32, out_gm, [row, col])\n```\n\n### Decision rule (from `pyasc-api-patterns` skill)\n- Loop reads any tile/scalar/accumulator a PREVIOUS iteration WROTE → `unroll_factor=2, gm_barrier=True`\n- No carried dependency (independent tiles, disjoint stores) → `unroll_factor=2` only (overlap enabled by default)\n\n---\n\n## 7. `asc2.atomic_add` — cross-core reduction pattern\n\nFile: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/atomic_ops.py` (lines 33–58)\n\n### Signature\n```python\n@require_jit\ndef atomic_add(src: LocalTensor, dst: GlobalTensor, offsets: Iterable[RuntimeInt]) -> None:\n```\n- `src`: `LocalTensor` (from UB; will be cast to UB automatically).\n- `dst`: `GlobalTensor`.\n- `offsets`: per-dim offsets into the global tensor.\n- **Supported dtypes**: `int16, int32, float16, bfloat16, float32`. `src.dtype` must equal `dst.dtype`.\n- Sibling ops: `asc2.atomic_max`, `asc2.atomic_min`.\n\n### Cross-core reduction pattern\nEach core computes a partial reduction locally, then atomically adds its partial result into a shared global location:\n```python\n# Conceptual pattern (per the docstring example):\nsrc = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [0], [128])         # local partial\nasc2.atomic_add(src, out_gm, [0])            # atomically add to global accumulator\n```\nFor a per-core partial-sum that must be combined across cores, point all cores' `atomic_add` calls at the same `offsets` (e.g. `[0]` for a single global scalar, or `[c0]` for per-channel). The hardware serialises the read-modify-writes.\n\n---\n\n## 8. `asc.ConstExpr` and `asc.GlobalAddress` — usage in kernel params\n\n### `asc.ConstExpr[T]` (and `asc2.ConstExpr`)\nFile: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc/language/core/constexpr.py` + `asc2/__init__.py:11`\n\n`asc2.ConstExpr` is re-exported from `asc.language.core.constexpr.ConstExpr` (so `asc.ConstExpr` and `asc2.ConstExpr` are the **same class**). It is a `Generic[T_co]` wrapper marking a parameter as compile-time-resolved. The concrete value is included in the JIT cache key.\n\n```python\nclass ConstExpr(Generic[T_co]):\n    value: T_co\n    def __init__(self, value): ...\n    @staticmethod\n    def unwrap(obj): ...   # returns obj.value if ConstExpr, else obj\n```\n\n#### When to use (from `constexpr-guide.md`)\n1. **Compile-time branching** — `if mode == 1:` only the matching branch is compiled.\n2. **Unsupported runtime types** — `str`, `tuple`, `list`, `dict` must be `ConstExpr`.\n3. **If-scoped variable fix** — when a variable is assigned in only one branch of an `if`.\n\n#### Usage in kernel params (from golden kernels)\n```python\n# rms_norm_f32.py:89-93 — note asc2.GlobalAddress AND asc2.ConstExpr:\n@asc2.jit(reuse_alloc=1)\ndef rms_norm_full_row_kernel(x_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress, gamma_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress,\n                             out_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress,\n                             num_rows: int,                          # runtime int\n                             num_cols: asc2.ConstExpr,                # compile-time (shape must be static)\n                             epsilon: asc.ConstExpr[float]):          # compile-time float\n```\n- `num_cols` is `ConstExpr` because it's used in the load shape `[1, num_cols]` — **load/copy_in shape values must be compile-time** (else `RuntimeError: All values in 'shape' must be integers`).\n- `epsilon` is `ConstExpr[float]`.\n- `asc2.ConstExpr` (no type arg) is also valid — used in `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:66-67` for `inv_count` and `eps`, and in `layer_norm_v4_f32.py:68` for `padded_cols`.\n\n### `asc.GlobalAddress` (and `asc2.GlobalAddress`)\nFile: `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc/language/core/ir_value.py:39-62` + `asc2/__init__.py:26`\n\n`asc2.GlobalAddress` is re-exported from `asc.language.core.ir_value.GlobalAddress`. It is the type of a global-memory pointer passed as a kernel argument. It supports `__add__(offset) -> GlobalAddress` (ptr+offset), enabling ranked sub-views:\n```python\nclass GlobalAddress(IRValue):\n    def __init__(self, handle, dtype=None): ...   # not called by user\n    @require_jit\n    def __add__(self, offset: RuntimeInt) -> GlobalAddress:\n        # emits emitasc.PtrOffsetOp (base + offset)\n```\n\n#### Usage in kernel params\n```python\n@asc2.jit\ndef kernel(x_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc2.GlobalAddress, ...):\n    x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])      # wrap pointer as GlobalTensor\n    ...\n```\nFor a sub-view at a runtime base offset (used in concat):\n```python\nin_s = asc2.global_tensor(in_ptr + B_i, [rows, w])   # GlobalAddress + offset\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. `asc.ceildiv` / `asc2.ceildiv` — usage\n\n### Two forms exist\n1. **`asc.ceildiv` (host-side helper)** — file `asc/language/core/utils.py:173`:\n   ```python\n   def ceildiv(x: int, y: int) -> int:\n       return (x + y - 1) // y\n   ```\n   Plain Python function, NOT a JIT op. Used on the **host** to compute launch params.\n\n2. **`asc2.ceildiv` (host AND kernel)** — file `asc2/language/utils.py:23-57`, re-exported at `asc2/__init__.py:56`:\n   ```python\n   @overload\n   def ceildiv(x: int, y: int) -> int: ...\n   @overload\n   def ceildiv(x: RuntimeInt, y: RuntimeInt) -> PlainValue: ...\n   @allow_jit\n   def ceildiv(x: RuntimeInt, y: RuntimeInt) -> RuntimeInt:\n       return (x + y - 1) // y\n   ```\n   Decorated with `@allow_jit` — **can be used BOTH on host and inside a `@asc2.jit` kernel**. Inside a kernel, follows C signed-integer ceiling-division semantics.\n\n### Usage (host-side, ubiquitous in golden kernels)\n```python\n# abs_f16.py:73-74:\nnum_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, TILE_SIZE)\nabs_kernel[CORE_NUM](x, out, size, TILE_SIZE, asc.ceildiv(num_tiles, CORE_NUM))\n\n# apply_adam_f32.py:97-99, tanh_f16.py:56-57, leaky_relu_f16.py:65-67 (same pattern)\n```\n\n### Usage inside a kernel (from the asc2.ceildiv docstring)\n```python\nnum_tiles = asc2.ceildiv(size, tile_size)\ntiles_per_core = asc2.ceildiv(num_tiles, asc2.block_num())\n```\nAlso: `PlainValue` has a `.ceildiv(other)` method (`ir_value.py:321`) that emits `CeilDivSI` — for runtime scalar ceiling division inside a kernel.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Scalar operations inside `@asc2.jit`\n\nScalars are represented as `PlainValue` (file `asc/language/core/ir_value.py:65-347`). PlainValue overloads all standard binary/unary/comparison operators, so **scalar arithmetic works naturally**.\n\n### `scalar_a / scalar_b` — YES, supported\n`PlainValue.__truediv__` (line 91) emits `DivSI` (int) or `DivF` (float). Reversed `__rtruediv__` also exists (line 144). So `1.0 / asc2.sqrt(...)` works:\n```python\n# rms_norm_f32.py:108 — 1.0 (Python float) / PlainValue (from asc2.sqrt):\ninv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)\n```\nHere `1.0` is materialised to a `PlainValue` via `materialize_ir_value`, then `__rtruediv__` is called.\n\n### `asc2.rsqrt(scalar)` — NO, not supported\n`asc2.rsqrt` only accepts `LocalTensor` (see §3). Workaround: `1.0 / asc2.sqrt(scalar)`.\n\n### `asc2.sqrt(scalar)` — YES, supported\n`asc2.sqrt` has `support_scalar=True` (unary_ops.py:242-246). Same for `asc2.exp` and `asc2.erf`. Other unary ops (`cos, sin, tan, sinh, cosh, tanh, log, log2, floor, ceil, abs, exp2, rsqrt, relu`) accept **tiles only**.\n\n### Full operator support on scalars (PlainValue)\n| Category | Operators |\n|---|---|\n| Binary | `+ - * / // % << >> & \\| ^` (`__pow__` raises NotImplementedError) |\n| Reversed binary | `__radd__`, `__rsub__`, `__rmul__`, `__rtruediv__`, `__rfloordiv__`, `__rmod__`, `__rlshift__`, `__rrshift__`, `__rand__`, `__ror__`, `__rxor__` |\n| Comparison | `== != >= > <= <` (return `PlainValue` of `int1`/`bit`) |\n| Unary | `-x` (neg), `+x` (pos). `~x` raises NotImplementedError. |\n| Methods | `.ceildiv(other)`, `.max(other)`, `.min(other)`, `.cast(dtype)`, `.logical_and/or/not()` |\n\n### Concrete scalar-ops example (RMSNorm full_row)\n```python\n# rms_norm_f32.py:107-108 — all scalar ops:\nsum_sq = asc2.reduce_sum(x_row_f32 * x_row_f32)   # PlainValue (scalar)\ninv_rms = 1.0 / asc2.sqrt(sum_sq / num_cols + epsilon)\n#        ^^^^   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n#        float  PlainValue.__rtruediv__(PlainValue from asc2.sqrt)\n#                     PlainValue.__truediv__ then __add__\n```\n\n### Constraints (from `pyasc-syntax-constraints` skill)\n- Built-in whitelist inside JIT: `dict, float, int, isinstance, issubclass, len, list, range, repr, str, tuple, type`.\n- `math.sqrt`/`math.*` inside JIT raises `RuntimeError: Unsupported function referenced`. Use `asc2.sqrt` inside the jit body, or precompute as a module-level Python constant.\n- `asc2.sqrt(0.5)` at module scope raises `AttributeError` — only valid inside a `@asc2.jit` body.\n- `break`, `continue`, early `return` from a kernel, `print`, `raise`, `try/except`, `yield`, `lambda`, `import`, nested functions, `global`, `nonlocal` are all **unsupported** inside `@asc2.jit`.\n- Augmented assignment (`+=`, `-=`, etc.) IS supported — that's how loop-carried accumulators work (`sum_sq = sum_sq + ...` is assignment, not `+=`, both work).\n\n---\n\n## Summary table — file locations for each API\n\n| API | Source file (current API) | Golden example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `asc2.range` | `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source/python/asc2/language/range.py` | `golden/kernels/rms_norm_f32.py:103,140`; `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:84` |\n| `asc2.reduce_sum` | `.../asc2/language/reduction_ops.py` | `rms_norm_f32.py:107,144`; `layer_norm_v4_f32.py:82,84`; `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:86-87` |\n| `asc2.rsqrt` | `.../asc2/language/unary_ops.py:222-227` | `layer_norm_v4_f32.py:86`; `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:91` (tile input only) |\n| `asc2.copy_in` / `copy_out` | `.../asc2/language/memory_ops.py` | `rms_norm_f32.py:105,113`; `layer_norm_v4_f32.py:79,93` |\n| `asc2.global_tensor` | `.../asc2/language/global_tensor.py` | `rms_norm_f32.py:99-101`; `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:68-73` |\n| Loop-carried accumulators | (pattern, not a single API) | `rms_norm_f32.py:136-144`; `layer_norm_v4_f32.py:117-125`; `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:80-87` |\n| `asc2.atomic_add` | `.../asc2/language/atomic_ops.py` | (no golden kernel uses it yet; docstring example in source) |\n| `asc.ConstExpr` / `asc2.ConstExpr` | `.../asc/language/core/constexpr.py`; re-exported `asc2/__init__.py:11` | `rms_norm_f32.py:92-93`; `batch_norm_v3_f32.py:66-67` |\n| `asc.GlobalAddress` / `asc2.GlobalAddress` | `.../asc/language/core/ir_value.py:39`; re-exported `asc2/__init__.py:26` | `rms_norm_f32.py:89-90` |\n| `asc.ceildiv` / `asc2.ceildiv` | `.../asc/language/core/utils.py:173` (host); `.../asc2/language/utils.py:23-57` (host+kernel) | `abs_f16.py:73`; `apply_adam_f32.py:97` |\n| Scalar ops | `.../asc/language/core/ir_value.py:65-347` (PlainValue) | `rms_norm_f32.py:108` (`1.0 / asc2.sqrt(...)`) |\n\n### Key discrepancy to remember\nThe `pyasc-api-patterns` skill doc (SKILL.md) and the older `docker/pyasc-overlay/` source use the **older API**: `asc2.tensor`, `asc2.load(gm, [shape], offsets=[...])`, `asc2.store(tile, gm, offsets=[...])`, and `asc2.range(..., parallel=True)`. The **current API** (used by all `golden/kernels/*.py` and the `pyasc-v2-source` tree) is: `asc2.global_tensor`, `asc2.copy_in(src, offsets, shape, ...)` (positional offsets), `asc2.copy_out(src, dst, offsets, ...)` (positional offsets), and `asc2.range(..., gm_barrier=True)`. Use the current API for new kernels.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_f9e8d1600ffeTOnADKxO6TVRTr","sessionId":"ses_f9e8b2c21ffeVgKgdpbNcnvj6Q","model":{"modelID":"glm-5.2","providerID":"dashscope"},"truncated":false},"title":"Search asc2 API patterns and constraints","time":{"start":1788341441504,"end":1788341788205}},"id":"prt_06174be9a001bBTXpXGglWVQO5","sessionID":"ses_f9e8d1600ffeTOnADKxO6TVRTr","messageID":"msg_061731270001jmLm0nEc2v35wc"}}
